<speak>
<p>Let u be any positive integer. Work with Gaussian integer coefficients on the cycle of length u squared. Each of the four polynomials H zero through H three has a positive or negative one at every coefficient position. Keep those same signs in all three normalized evaluations: c is half the sum of all four polynomials; d reverses the signs on H one and H three; and g uses the real difference of H zero and H two, plus i times the difference of H one and H three, all divided by two. Subtract c from d and from g. The H zero terms cancel in both differences. Our divisions will be exact, before any coefficient reduction.<break time="1000ms"/></p>
<p>First, subtract the two real formulas. The difference d minus c is negative H one minus H three. Dividing by two gives negative one half of the sum of H one and H three.<break time="700ms"/>For the second quotient, the difference g minus c has numerator negative H one minus twice H two minus H three, plus i times the difference of H one and H three, over two. To divide by one plus i, multiply by one minus i over two. Expanding the numerator gives real part negative twice H two minus twice H three, and imaginary part twice H one plus twice H two, all over four. Simplify to negative one half of the sum of H two and H three, plus i times one half of the sum of H one and H two.<break time="700ms"/>Each half-sum has integer coefficients: two signs add to negative two, zero, or two, and their halves are negative one, zero, or one. Therefore the first quotient is an integer polynomial, and the second is a Gaussian integer polynomial. Both divisions are justified before reduction.<break time="1000ms"/></p>
<p>Now define E as the sum of the two integral quotients. Add their exact formulas. The two terms negative H three over two combine to negative H three. The remaining real terms are negative one half of the sum of H one and H two; the imaginary terms are i times that same half-sum. Collect the common factor i minus one. We obtain E equal to negative H three, plus the factor i minus one times the half-sum of H one and H two.<break time="700ms"/>This isolates the correction term. Since i minus one equals i times the quantity one plus i, that correction will vanish in the later reduction. We have not performed that reduction here. Retain the two exact divisibilities as well: two divides d minus c, and one plus i divides g minus c. These calculations use the common signs alone. They require neither norm identities nor oddness of u.<break time="1200ms"/></p>
</speak>
